        '6-8', $q$How Glaciers Shape the Land$q$, $q$(1) A glacier is a huge, slow-moving body of ice that forms on land. (2) Glaciers begin when more snow falls each winter than melts each summer. (3) Over many years, the layers of snow press down and turn into thick, solid ice. (4) When the ice becomes heavy enough, it begins to slide downhill under its own weight.

(5) As a glacier moves, it changes the land beneath it. (6) Rocks frozen into the bottom of the ice scrape the ground like sandpaper. (7) This process, called abrasion, can carve deep scratches into solid rock. (8) Glaciers also pick up boulders and carry them for many miles. (9) My cousin went skiing on a snowy mountain last winter and loved it.

(10) Glaciers leave clear signs behind when they melt. (11) Some valleys that glaciers carved are shaped like the letter U instead of the letter V. (12) Piles of rock and soil that the ice dropped, called moraines, form long ridges. (13) Many lakes fill the deep holes that glaciers dug into the ground.$q$, 'draft')
