        '6-8', $q$Papyrus: The Paper of Ancient Egypt$q$, $q$(1) Long before modern paper existed, people in ancient Egypt wrote on a material called papyrus. (2) Papyrus is a tall, grassy plant that grew along the banks of the Nile River. (3) Its stems could reach about fifteen feet high. (4) The Egyptians used this plant to make sheets that were light, smooth and easy to carry.

(5) Making a sheet of papyrus took several steps. (6) Workers peeled the stems and cut the soft inside into thin strips. (7) They laid the strips side by side, and then they placed a second layer across the first. (8) The layers were pressed hard and left to dry in the sun. (9) The plant's natural juices acted like glue and held the strips together. (10) Finally, the dry sheet was polished with a smooth stone.

(11) Papyrus was useful for many kinds of writing. (12) Scribes recorded taxes, letters, stories and medical advice. (13) Sheets could be joined end to end to make long scrolls. (14) Papyrus is really cool. (15) Because the air in Egypt is so dry, some papyrus scrolls have lasted for thousands of years.$q$, 'draft')
